  {:id :H1 :category :update :title "Signed update download and apply"
   :weight 3 :required true :required-level :release
   :procedure "Publish an update; the machine fetches, verifies and applies it."
   :expected "The new version runs; an unsigned update is refused."
   :needs "A receipt or log and a screenshot or readback at the required level, kept with the run."
   :status :pass :qemu-progress :pass
   :evidence ["receipt os/aiueos/qualification/receipts/vm-20261025/h1-signed-update-apply/receipt.edn"]
   :note "Measured 2026-10-25: an update volume (UPDATE.ID, UPDATE.MNF, UPDATE.SIG + the three boot-chain files) is read by the loader, the manifest is verified with a C RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5-SHA256 implementation under the baked release key, each file's sha256 is checked against the manifest, and the files are applied to the system ESP with read-back compare (AIUEOS_UPDATE_APPLIED). A manifest with one flipped byte is refused before any write (AIUEOS_UPDATE_REFUSED_unsigned) and the untouched system keeps booting. Designed 2026-10-25 (reusable parts measured this session): the update package is the three boot-chain files plus UPDATE.MANIFEST (sha256 per file) and UPDATE.MANIFEST.SIG (RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5-SHA256 under the release key). The loader already has SHA-256 file verification and the FAT write machinery proven by H3 (READ|WRITE|CREATE+ARCHIVE, read-back compare); the kernel already has the signed-payload admission kotoba_aiueos_recovery_payload_admission (pci.c:386, kotoba-sha256 + kotoba-rsa2048-pkcs1 + public-key policy) whose verify object can be linked into the loader the way the kernel links it. Detection mirrors RECOVERY.ID: an update volume carries UPDATE.ID. Apply copies the verified files to the system ESP (the H3 copy path); an absent/failed manifest signature is AIUEOS_UPDATE_REFUSED_unsigned with no writes. The 'fetch' in the QEMU test is the update volume (the physical-machine story is a network fetch); the signed-manifest generator reuses sign-release-image.py's PKCS#1 signing. Measured 2026-10-25 (toolchain finding): rotating the kotoba rsa2048.o's baked admission key to the release key is BLOCKED — the committed objects call kernel-load-u8-4k (ADR 0373 window ops) which no available compiler (the amu checkout binary, the amu-1560 F2.elf) knows as a form (E2101), so the object cannot be rebuilt outside the original build environment. The chosen route is therefore a small C RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5-SHA256 verify in the loader (same algorithm as scripts/verify-release-signature.py) with the RELEASE public key baked as constants — the matching private key is held by the signing pipeline (qualification/release-key/), so update manifests can be signed in the test. The kernel's kotoba_aiueos_recovery_payload_admission stays as-is for the initramfs path."
   }
